  <h2>An AI film director, in everyone's <em>pocket.</em></h2></div>

  <div class="reveal">
  <p>For a hundred years, the ability to make something cinematic was gated. Gated by equipment you couldn't afford, by crews you couldn't hire, by studios you couldn't book, and by a language — shots, light, sound, rhythm — that took years of film school to learn. A real film took crews, studios, and a career's worth of permission most people would never get.</p>
  <p>And yet a billion people already make video every single day. They cut TikToks at 2am. They have the instinct, the taste, the eye, the stories worth telling. What they don't have is the crew, the gear, or the decade of vocabulary that separates a clip from a <em>film</em>.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal"><p class="pull">The camera was never the missing piece. The <em>director</em> was.</p></div>

  <div class="reveal">
  <p>The phone already in your hand shoots in 4K. The thing standing between that phone and something genuinely cinematic was always the person behind it who knows what to do — where to stand, how to move, when the light is right, what the edit should feel like.</p>
  <p>Lumira <strong>is that person.</strong> Not a template. Not a filter. A director you talk to — one who understands brand, story, and light. You tell it what you're making; it asks the questions a real director would, then tells you exactly where to stand, how to hold the phone, what lens move to make, and when the rose catches the sun. It directs you, shot by shot, in plain language. Then it grades the footage like an A24 colorist while you sleep.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal">
  <div class="term">
    <div class="bar"><i class="r"></i><i class="y"></i><i class="g"></i><span>lumira — live</span></div>
<pre><span class="out">Lumira</span>  Scene 3. Push in slowly as she
        turns — grief, not drama. Hold
        it two seconds longer than feels
        comfortable.

<span class="cm">you</span>     [films]

<span class="out">Lumira</span>  That's the take. I'll crush the
        blacks and add 16mm grain in post.</pre>
  </div>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal">
  <p>Here is the part that matters. If everyone who already loves making video suddenly had a director in their pocket, the number of people who can make something truly cinematic does not double. It goes from thousands to <strong>billions</strong>.</p>
  <p>That is not a better editing app. That is the <em>democratization of an entire art form</em> — the same shift that happened when the camera phone made everyone a photographer, except this time it's filmmaking. The instinct is already everywhere. People love telling stories with video; they're just trapped inside fifteen-second clips because nobody ever handed them the craft.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="reveal"><p class="pull">The next great filmmaker is making TikToks right now. Lumira is how we <em>find them.</em></p></div>

  <div class="reveal">
  <p>I think that's world-changing in the most literal sense. Give a generation that already thinks in video the ability to make real films, and the culture doesn't shift at the edges — it moves at the center. New voices that never had access to a studio. Stories from places Hollywood never points a camera. A hundred years of gatekeeping, dissolved by the device already in everyone's hand.</p>
  <p>That's why Lumira exists. Not to make ads cheaper — though it does. To take the single most powerful storytelling medium ever invented and hand it to <strong>everyone.</strong></p>
  </div>

